Binding & Storage Allocation
6. Worked Example
The example below combines a static local variable with a pointer parameter, showing binding and
storage allocation working together. #include <stdio.h>
// 'history' is static: storage is allocated once, before main() runs
void recordAndDouble(int *value) {
  static int callHistory = 0;
  callHistory++;
  // 'value' is a stack-dynamic pointer parameter
  // it holds the address of a variable owned by the caller
  *value = *value * 2;
  printf("Call #%d -> new value: %d\n", callHistory, *value);
}
int main() {
  int score = 5;
  recordAndDouble(&score);  // score becomes 10
  recordAndDouble(&score);  // score becomes 20
  printf("Final score: %d\n", score);
  return 0;
}
Output
Call #1 -> new value: 10
Call #2 -> new value: 20
Final score: 20
Here, callHistory keeps counting across both calls because it has static storage, exactly like the count
variable in the earlier trackCalls() example.

Local Referencing & Parameter Passing
Local referencing describes how variables declared inside a function are created, stored, and
destroyed during program execution. C provides two main kinds of local variables — stack-dynamic
(normal) variables and static local variables — which differ in how long their storage persists. Static vs. Normal Local Variables
A normal local variable is allocated on the call stack each time its enclosing function is invoked, and it is
destroyed when the function returns. A static local variable, in contrast, is allocated once in a fixed
memory region and retains its value across multiple calls to the function. #include <stdio.h>
void trackCalls()
{
  // 1. Static local variable
  // It is initialized only ONCE when the program starts. static int count = 0;
  // 2. Normal (stack-dynamic) variable
  // It is recreated every time the function is called.

= 0;
  // 2. Normal (stack-dynamic) variable
  // It is recreated every time the function is called. int localCount = 0;
  count++;      // This persists
  localCount++; // This resets to 0 every time
  printf("Static Count: %d | Normal Count: %d\n", count, localCount);
}
int main()
{
  trackCalls(); // Output: Static Count: 1 | Normal Count: 1
  trackCalls(); // Output: Static Count: 2 | Normal Count: 1
  trackCalls(); // Output: Static Count: 3 | Normal Count: 1
  return 0;
}
Output
Static Count: 1 | Normal Count: 1
Static Count: 2 | Normal Count: 1
Static Count: 3 | Normal Count: 1

