Inside function: 110
Outside function: 10

Local Referencing & Parameter Passing
2. Pass by Reference (Using Pointers)
C doesn't have a "true" pass-by-reference like some other languages, so we use pointers. Instead of
sending the value, we send the address (where the variable lives in memory). This allows the function
to reach back into the caller's memory and modify the original variable directly, rather than working on a
disposable copy. #include <stdio.h>
// This function receives the ADDRESS of the number
void reallyChange(int *ptr) {
  *ptr = *ptr + 100; // Go to that address and change the value
}
int main() {
  int myNumber = 10;
  reallyChange(&myNumber); // Send the address of myNumber
  // Now it is 110 because we edited the original memory cell
  printf("After pointer change: %d\n", myNumber);
  return 0;
}
Output
After pointer change: 110
Why This Works
When &myNumber is passed, the function receives the memory address of myNumber, stored in the
pointer ptr.
